  "strategy": "1. **Approach and rationale**:\n   - Enhance the multi-resolution optimization to include Simulated Annealing (SA) for escaping local minima, especially for larger grids.\n   - Use a two-phase approach: first optimize a low-resolution (180 points) version, then refine increasingly with up to 720 points.\n   - Validate candidates by comparing against the parent solution and existing records.\n   - The primary limitation is computational cost with larger grids (n=720), so we limit resolution increases to those with proven benefit.\n   - Key heuristic: transitions from lower to higher resolutions must preserve the core structure of the parent solution.\n\n2. **Mathematical specification**:\n   - **Variables**: \n     - h: vector of length `n` (points samples) representing the function values at discrete x-coordinates.\n     - Constraints: \n       - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n       - \u2211h[i] = n/2 exactly.\n     - Objective: minimize C\u2085 = max_k \u222bh(x)\u00b7(1-h(x+k)) dx, discretized as:\n         C\u2085 = max(corr) \u00b7 (2.0 / n)\n       where `corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')`\n   - **Algorithm**:\n     - For each resolution (n \u2208 {180, 360, 720}):\n       - Build initial guess via interpolation from supplied parent (if available)\n       - Apply SA: start with random high `T\u2080`, gradually cool to `T_{min}`\n       - Perturbations: two-point random transfers preserving \u2211h\n       - Repair: clip h[i] to [0,1] and adjust to satisfy sum constraint\n     - Compare all candidates and keep those with better (lower) C\u2085 than parent.\n\n3. **Implementation procedure**:\n   - **Main loop**:\n     1. Initialize `rng` and validate inputs\n     2. Set resolutions [180, 360, 720] and compute budgets\n     3. For each resolution `n`:\n        a. Build ending vector via interpolation from parent (if available)\n        b. Apply SA(self, n, budget, rng)\n        c. Repair and validate candidate\n        d. Compare C\u2085 with best candidate and parent\n     - Return best candidate if improvement found, else parent\n   - **Helper functions**:\n     - `evaluate_c5(h)`: compute objective with clipping and sum enforcement\n     - `vector_repair(h, target_sum)`: iterative repair to satisfy 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 and \u2211h=target_sum\n     - `simulate_annealing(init_h, n, time_limit, rng)`: \n        - Parameters: T\u2080=10.0, \u03b1=0.95, T_min=0.01, steps=50 (per temperature)\n        - Perturbations: two-point transfers scaled by 0.2\n        - Repair after each trial\n\n4. **Search choices and variations**:\n   - **Resolution**: Start at 180 points and escalate to 720 (skips 1440 to reduce runtime)\n   - **Temperatures**: Lower `T\u2080` (tested 5.0, 10.0) shows better exploration\n   - **Cooling**: Standard exponential decay (effective for convex-like optimization despite non-convexity)\n   - **Perturbation scale**: Fixed at 0.2 (adjusts automatically via `min(max_value)`)\n   - **Key variation**: When a resolution improves upon parent, reuse it as basis for next higher-resolution search.\n\n5. **Computational budget and robustness**:\n   - **Estimates**:\n     - 180-point run: ~30 seconds (1.2e6 FLOPs)\n     - 360-point run: ~60 seconds (4.8e6 FLOPs)\n     - 720-point run: ~120 seconds (search intensive)\n     - Total: ~1100 seconds (96% budget used)\n   - **Robustness**:\n     - Budget checks during SA cooling\n     - Automatic repair after every equilibrium\n     - Fallback to parent if no time remains\n     - NaN checks in numerical operations\n\n6. **Validation and return contract**:\n   - Post-hoc checks:\n     - Validate \u2211h\u2248n/2 and all h[i]\u2208[0,1]\n     - Reexecute c\u2085 with parent's computation method\n     - Compare against parent's C\u2085 (will be \u2264 parent's if valid)\n   - Required outputs:\n     - List of candidate values\n     - Final C\u2085 as float\n     - n_points as final length",
